WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.680
If you gradually build up your
stamina, you can go a long way.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.270
And the same with language learning.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.400
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.950
And, uh, today I want to talk about
language learning and entrepreneurship

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.060
and how they're connected.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.670
And, uh, I would remind you if you
enjoy these videos, please subscribe,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.530
click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.430
Uh, and if you are on Apple
Podcasts, please don't hesitate

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.900
to leave a review there.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.140
So, you know, for much of my life, I
have been, I guess, an entrepreneur

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.720
and a learner of languages.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.420
So both are very much a part of me.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.260
Uh, I started out as a Canadian diplomat
for seven years, but then for most

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.320
of the remainder of the period, I
either worked for a large corporation

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:54.780
or, starting in 1987, I started my
own company in the wood business.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.195
And of course, 15 years ago or
so can't remember exactly, uh,

00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:02.465
my son Mark and I started LingQ.

00:01:03.975 --> 00:01:09.495
So, you know, as I reflect back on this,
I see some real connections between

00:01:10.455 --> 00:01:12.735
entrepreneurship and language learning.

00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:19.035
The biggest connection is that both
require you are and very much a part of

00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:23.535
both these activities is creating value.

00:01:23.535 --> 00:01:26.115
And I can explain what I mean by that.

00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:30.405
Now, when we say entrepreneur,
it's a word, at least the way I

00:01:30.405 --> 00:01:32.535
interpret it comes from French...

00:01:33.105 --> 00:01:38.085
to undertake something, to do something.

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:43.110
And I think doing something,
you know, Undertaking something.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:48.450
Being an entrepreneur is a big
part of being a human being.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.820
We have ideas, we have enthusiasm,
we have curiosity energy, and we

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.490
channel that into some activity,
hopefully a useful activity.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.980
And where language learning and,
and entrepreneurship come, come

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.990
together is around this idea of value.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:12.045
If you are going to be a successful
language learner, You have to believe

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:14.715
that learning a language is valuable.

00:02:15.225 --> 00:02:20.595
And so many times I will sit there
and if I'm watching a movie that I can

00:02:20.595 --> 00:02:25.334
understand, or I engage with someone
in a language that I've learned or

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:29.535
I'm visiting that country, or I'm just
sitting back with a book and sort of

00:02:29.535 --> 00:02:34.245
reaping the benefits of the effort that
I have put into learning that language.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:37.214
I get a tremendous feeling of value.

00:02:37.214 --> 00:02:38.415
This is very valuable.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:43.560
When I'm, um, learning Persian now,
as I'm doing it, I learned about the

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:49.620
history of Persia and that, or even I
follow a current events in Iran, uh,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.220
in the Persian language, in Farsi.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.450
And then I look up Khuzestan, which
is very much in the news now in Iran.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.010
And I find out that it's a very
rich area with, with oil and water

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.570
resources and farming is open.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.555
So all of that brings me value.

00:03:06.555 --> 00:03:10.065
My activity brings me value, but
in the case of language learning,

00:03:10.275 --> 00:03:12.375
you're bringing value to yourself.

00:03:13.155 --> 00:03:14.415
With entrepreneurship.

00:03:14.505 --> 00:03:19.875
I think the key to entrepreneurship
is to provide value to other people.

00:03:21.045 --> 00:03:25.665
Uh, only if you provide value to other
people, are you going to be successful.

00:03:26.655 --> 00:03:31.665
Uh, other similarities are in my
experience when I started my own

00:03:31.665 --> 00:03:36.105
company in the wood business in
1987, uh, you know, I had spent

00:03:36.105 --> 00:03:38.415
a long time listening to people.

00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:39.945
I worked for a large corporation.

00:03:40.275 --> 00:03:43.155
I had spoken to many people
in the Japanese wood business.

00:03:43.335 --> 00:03:49.575
I knew what certain people were looking
for and what was not being provided

00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:54.585
by the major Canadian or other foreign
exporters to the Japanese market.

00:03:54.975 --> 00:03:59.115
So I had to absorb, I had acquired a
lot of information by being patient

00:03:59.835 --> 00:04:05.805
and then armed with that knowledge,
I was able to put together some, you

00:04:05.805 --> 00:04:11.234
know, supply and sizes that were not
otherwise available in the quality

00:04:11.234 --> 00:04:13.035
that was not otherwise available.

00:04:13.215 --> 00:04:17.204
I was able to provide something
of value to the market.

00:04:17.985 --> 00:04:23.865
Now, something else I think that's similar
between language learning and, uh, and

00:04:23.865 --> 00:04:29.715
entrepreneurship is when you start out
on your own, and as I've done now twice,

00:04:30.195 --> 00:04:36.740
uh, in the lumber business and then in
language, It's a long road and you have

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:42.469
some initial successes, uh, but they
can be almost sort of a mirage because

00:04:42.469 --> 00:04:44.930
in both cases it takes a long time.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:50.820
It takes a long time to build up to that
point where say in the case of languages

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:56.360
you're getting that value that enjoyment,
uh, you know, it's like, um, I dunno,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.550
it's almost like it's a long distance run.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.210
It's a, you know, here in Vancouver we
have a thing called the Grouse Grind,

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.155
which is somewhat less than an hour
climbing up a very steep mountain.

00:05:07.545 --> 00:05:09.885
If you start too quickly, you're
going to burn yourself out.

00:05:10.785 --> 00:05:14.505
Uh, if you're going to be in a
long distance race and you start

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:16.305
too quickly, you run out of gas.

00:05:16.935 --> 00:05:22.005
And whereas if you gradually build up
your stamina, you can go a long way.

00:05:22.275 --> 00:05:23.625
And the same with language learning.

00:05:23.925 --> 00:05:26.745
It's not what you can do in the
first little while that matters

00:05:26.745 --> 00:05:30.065
it's what you can do, you know, at
the end of the journey or later on.

00:05:30.915 --> 00:05:33.635
And the same is true, uh, the
companies, I mean, whether  it be

00:05:33.635 --> 00:05:38.415
LingQ or whether it be, uh, my wood
business, it was a long road before

00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:40.395
we started to get any traction.

00:05:40.485 --> 00:05:44.025
We had some initial successes,
but that wasn't enough to

00:05:44.025 --> 00:05:46.275
make these ventures viable.

00:05:46.365 --> 00:05:51.555
We had to stay with it, stay with it
long enough for it to finally mature.

00:05:52.305 --> 00:05:57.990
Uh, another thing about entrepreneurship,
which I firmly believe, is that, you

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.580
know, obviously you have to give value.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:00.990
So it begins with giving.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:06.540
You can't begin by taking you begin
by giving and you also have to be,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.680
in most cases, that's not always the
case, but I think largely you have to

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.500
be a man of, or a woman of your word.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.230
If you say you're going to do
something, you have to do it.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:20.190
You actually have to deliver
in entrepreneurship so

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.780
that people will trust you.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.560
And that means obviously
knowing your business.

00:06:26.354 --> 00:06:31.515
Not committing to do things that you
can't do, uh, being honest and so

00:06:31.515 --> 00:06:34.005
forth and so on and staying with it.

00:06:34.244 --> 00:06:36.344
And I think language
learning is the same way.

00:06:36.344 --> 00:06:37.994
You have to be honest with yourself.

00:06:38.025 --> 00:06:42.315
You can't tell yourself that you're
better than you are or criticize

00:06:42.315 --> 00:06:44.864
yourself for being worse than you are.

00:06:45.135 --> 00:06:50.414
You just have to stay the course,
honestly, and eventually you will

00:06:51.164 --> 00:06:54.705
reap the rewards and the same I
have always found in business.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:07:00.734
If you are honest with your counterparts
in terms of what you can do without

00:07:00.765 --> 00:07:05.984
overstating, what you can do, but
also not doubting that you can do it.

00:07:06.104 --> 00:07:08.565
In other words, you have to have
the confidence that you can do it

00:07:08.685 --> 00:07:10.815
without overstating what you can do.

00:07:11.234 --> 00:07:15.405
On that basis you will, in time
because it doesn't happen overnight,

00:07:16.005 --> 00:07:19.755
and if it's based on your knowledge,
knowledge of the market, knowledge

00:07:19.755 --> 00:07:24.060
of products, eventually knowledge
of people, you will build them: the

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.550
contacts, the credibility and so forth.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.600
And similarly in language learning, if
you give yourself credit for what you

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:35.910
have achieved, don't overstate what
you can do, you will find that you

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.230
will gain more and more confidence,
and eventually you will be able to, you

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.240
know, reap the rewards of those efforts.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.260
So I wanted to mention that today
because I think, uh, I might just

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.610
over the next little while in these
videos, talk a bit about my experience

00:07:51.405 --> 00:07:55.695
as an entrepreneur, things that have
worked for me, uh, I don't know if that

00:07:55.695 --> 00:07:58.755
would be of interest to you out there.

00:07:59.205 --> 00:08:01.995
Uh, I'm also going to be
talking in different languages.

00:08:01.995 --> 00:08:07.065
I haven't done that for awhile because
I think most people probably understand

00:08:07.065 --> 00:08:09.914
English, not certain that they all
understand these other languages.

00:08:10.275 --> 00:08:14.115
Uh, if I do speak in other languages,
we will make sure they are translated.

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:20.655
Uh, when I speak in English, The
video and the audio and the text

00:08:20.655 --> 00:08:22.825
are uploaded to LingQ as lessons.

00:08:23.315 --> 00:08:28.474
Uh, we have had a policy at LingQ that
we really only want native speaker

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:30.724
content in, uh, you know, the library.

00:08:30.724 --> 00:08:33.875
So if I speak in another language,
I'm obviously not a native speaker,

00:08:34.204 --> 00:08:38.314
I'm not sure it's appropriate to have
that as content in our libraries.

00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:44.105
Uh, but at any rate, uh, that's kind of a
kickoff to this idea of talking a little

00:08:44.105 --> 00:08:48.165
bit more about entrepreneurship as it
connects to languages or even without

00:08:48.165 --> 00:08:50.415
any particular connection to languages.

00:08:50.775 --> 00:08:55.695
And so I'll be interested in hearing
from you what you think of this idea.

00:08:55.995 --> 00:08:57.155
So thank you for listening.

00:08:57.535 --> 00:08:57.915
Bye for now.

